      {
        "surveyID": "109100",
        "surveyDate": "1996-06-17",
        "narrative": "<p>Lake One is in Ecological Lake Class 7, which consists of 41 lakes in northeast Minnesota that are large, have very irregularly shaped shorelines, and have very soft and infertile water.  Lake One is smaller and has a more irregular shoreline than most lakes in this class.  Lake One stratifies thermally in midsummer and there is poor oxygen below 40-45 ft.  The shoreline is mostly boulder and there are few aquatic plants.  The Kawishiwi River flows through Lake One; there is a rapids at the outlet that prevents upstream fish movement.The total catch of fish (all species combined) in the gillnets in 1996 of 21.2 fish/net was lower than normal for this lake class, but similar to previous catches in this lake.  Lake Class 7 is one of the more productive classes, in terms of fish biomass, in northeast Minnesota.  Lake One, like some other flowage-type lakes in the area, does not produce as many fish as similar lakes without large rivers flowing through them.Fish populations in 1996 were dominated by northern pike and white sucker.Northern pike numbers in 1996 of 5.4/gillnet were higher than normal for this lake class and were also higher than the long term average catch in this lake.  Pike sizes averaged 20 in, which is somewhat small for this lake class; the largest pike was 37 in (12 lb).  Growth of young pike (17 in at age three) was average by area standards.  Larger pike probably grow faster when they get to a size where they can eat the cisco that are present in this lake.Walleye numbers in 1996 of 2.1/gillnet were below average for this lake class, but were similar to the long term average catch in this lake.  Walleye sizes averaged 12 in, which is smaller than average for this lake class and smaller than in past assessments on this lake.  Walleye growth (12 in at age four) was slower than average for the area.Sucker numbers in 1996 were average for this lake class.  Bluegill numbers were average; bluegill sizes averaged 7 in, with 40 percent 8 in or larger.  Cisco numbers were average, but the population is probably larger than indicated because few nets were set in deep, cold water where cisco live.  Cisco sizes were small, averaging 8 in; the largest was 11 in.</p>",
